WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.934
The payment service appends a 4 kilobyte authorization record to its log with a write call.

2
00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.880
The call returns in about ten microseconds.

3
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.387
Is the record on disk?

4
00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:14.134
Not yet.

5
00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:22.578
The kernel copied it into the page cache, its copy of file data in RAM, and marked the page dirty: newer than the disk.

6
00:00:22.578 --> 00:00:23.784
Then it returned.

7
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.412
The kernel's flusher thread writes dirty pages later.

8
00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:32.991
Once a page has been dirty for 30 seconds, the flusher writes it at its next wake up.

9
00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:34.172
This is writeback.

10
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.857
Record 2 is a refund.

11
00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:40.427
Its write call returns just as fast, and the new page is dirty.

12
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.090
Now the power goes out.

13
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.553
The page cache was RAM, so it's wiped.

14
00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:50.839
Record 2 is gone, though its write returned success, and the caller may already have heard saved.

15
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.163
So the service calls fsync before it answers.

16
00:00:54.163 --> 00:01:03.792
The kernel writes the file's dirty pages, tells the SSD to flush its own cache, and returns only when the device says the data is stored.

17
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:06.709
That costs milliseconds, not microseconds.

18
00:01:06.709 --> 00:01:15.329
In the lab, forcing after every record took 2 to 9 milliseconds, so one thread can make only a few hundred records a second durable.

19
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.545
Group commit shares that cost.

20
00:01:17.545 --> 00:01:20.477
While one fsync runs, more requests arrive.

21
00:01:20.477 --> 00:01:26.955
The service writes all their records, calls fsync once, then answers every caller in the group.

22
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:32.537
Forcing once per 100 records brings the cost down to tens of microseconds a record.

23
00:01:32.537 --> 00:01:38.005
PostgreSQL commits work the same way: concurrent commits share one flush of the log.

24
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:41.448
A write returns when the kernel has your bytes.

25
00:01:41.448 --> 00:01:43.979
fsync returns when the disk has them.

26
00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:48.152
Answer only after fsync, and let many records share each one.
